// ---------- Abstract ----------
children.push(
  h1(T`Abstract`),
  mix([
    T`Background and aims: Milk tea (typically 35–50 g sugar and 100–200 mg caffeine per serving) has never been prospectively studied for atrial fibrillation (AF) and is not instrumented in genome-wide association studies (GWAS). We tested whether genetically proxied intake of its sweetened-beverage components is causally associated with AF.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Methods and results: Seven beverage exposures were instrumented in UK Biobank GWAS (N = 64,949–447,485); AF statistics came from Nielsen 2018 (60,620 cases; primary), non-overlapping FinnGen R13 (67,783 cases; co-primary), AFGen 2018 (sensitivity) and BioBank Japan (East Asian context). Inverse-variance-weighted estimates were complemented by robust estimators, multivariable MR, locus-level colocalization and cross-trait LD-score regression. No sweetened-beverage exposure showed evidence of a causal association with AF (European meta-analytic odds ratios 0.82–0.97; P ≥ 0.06). A nominal coffee association (OR 1.34, 1.11–1.62; P = 0.002) was not robust: null weighted-median estimates, source-dependent outlier robustness, attenuation after body-mass-index adjustment (OR 0.82; P = 0.099) and colocalization only at the adiposity locus FTO. Sweetened-beverage traits nonetheless showed positive genetic correlations with AF (rg = 0.24–0.31; FDR q < 0.05).`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Conclusion: Genetically proxied sweetened-beverage intake showed no evidence of a causal association with AF; moderate-to-large effects are excluded, but small effects are not. The modest observational association likely reflects adiposity-related confounding; direct milk-tea studies in East Asian cohorts remain needed.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Keywords: Mendelian randomization; atrial fibrillation; sugar-sweetened beverages; coffee; genetic correlation; causal inference`,
  ])
);

// ---------- Introduction ----------
children.push(
  h1(T`Introduction`),
  mix([
    T`Atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most common sustained cardiac arrhythmia and a major cause of ischaemic stroke, heart failure and premature death; identifying modifiable lifestyle determinants, including dietary factors, is a public-health priority [1].`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Bubble (milk) tea—typically 35–50 g of added sugar and 100–200 mg of caffeine per serving, often with non-dairy creamer or high-fat toppings—is among the most widely consumed prepared beverages of young East Asians and is spreading worldwide. No prospective cohort has measured milk tea directly and no GWAS instruments it; milk-tea-type beverages motivated, but were not directly evaluated in, this analysis.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`The closest available evidence concerns sugar-sweetened beverages (SSBs): in 201,856 UK Biobank participants, >2 L/week of SSBs or artificially sweetened beverages (ASBs) was associated with 10% and 20% higher incident AF risk [2], confirmed in prediabetes and diabetes [3]; residual confounding cannot be excluded. Previous Mendelian randomization (MR) analyses examined single beverages in smaller outcome resources, without adiposity modelling or East Asian context [8,29,40].`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`For caffeine, the second major bioactive component of milk tea, habitual coffee was inversely associated with incident AF in large cohorts [4,5,6] and a meta-analysis [7]; an MR analysis found no causal coffee–AF association [8]; and the randomized DECAF trial found no increase in AF recurrence [9]. A 2026 American Heart Association scientific statement reached the same conclusion and prioritized disentangling caffeine from beverage co-ingredients [10].`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Milk tea itself has never been studied as an exposure; the SSB–AF association lacks causal evidence; and sugar and caffeine have rarely been examined within one causal framework. Leveraging UK Biobank beverage-consumption GWAS [11] and the largest public AF GWAS, we conducted two-sample and multivariable MR of sweetened beverage consumption (not milk tea itself) and AF, aiming (i) to estimate causal effects of sweetened-beverage intake, particularly sugar added to tea or coffee (the closest genetic proxy for milk-tea-type beverages); (ii) to disentangle sugar- and caffeine-related behaviour with body mass index (BMI) modelled jointly; (iii) to examine consistency in an East Asian population; and (iv) to benchmark sensitivity with alcohol as a positive control.`,
  ])
);

