// ---------- Discussion ----------
children.push(
  h1(T`Discussion`),
  h2(T`Principal findings`),
  mix([
    T`In this two-sample and multivariable Mendelian randomization study using the largest public European-ancestry AF GWAS, genetically proxied consumption of sweetened beverages showed no evidence of a causal association with atrial fibrillation, and the null estimates exclude moderate-to-large but not small effects. The null held across five distinct exposures—including sugar added to tea or coffee, the closest genetic proxy for milk-tea-type beverages—three European outcome sources and all complementary estimators; three nominal IVW signals emerged, but none survived scrutiny.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Comparison with previous work`),
  mix([
    T`Beyond previous single-beverage and dietary-exposure MR analyses [8,29,40,41], this study provides a unified multi-exposure framework: five sweetened-beverage phenotypes and their caffeinated counterparts against the largest public AF GWAS, with adiposity modelled jointly, locus-level colocalization and an East Asian contextual arm. Our nulls diverge from UK Biobank cohort estimates of 10–20% higher incident AF risk among high SSB/ASB consumers [2,3], and from a smaller MR reporting a positive SSB–AF association (OR 1.023; P = 0.0039) [29] using an outcome GWAS one-sixteenth the size of ours (3,818 cases), a relaxed instrument threshold and no adiposity modelling; our multivariable, colocalization and CAUSE analyses argue against a causal interpretation of that signal. The divergence is plausibly reconciled by residual confounding—our analyses show adiposity accounting for the only nominal signal—by MR's lifelong-average estimand versus mid-life cohort exposure, and by limited power for small effects.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Instrument specificity and horizontal pleiotropy`),
  mix([
    T`Genetic instruments for self-reported beverage intake are not molecule-specific: they index intake behaviour together with its adiposity, dietary-quality and socioeconomic correlates, so horizontal pleiotropy is the expectation rather than the exception for traits of this kind [32,33]. Three features of the design mitigate this concern. First, such pleiotropy manufactures false-positive signals, not precise false negatives: the headline estimates are null with confidence bounds that exclude the observational effect sizes, an improbable pattern were a moderate causal effect present. Second, the single nominal signal that did arise was dissected rather than accepted—source-dependent outlier sensitivity, attenuation once BMI was modelled jointly, and colocalization confined to the adiposity locus FTO show the framework detecting and isolating pleiotropy rather than concealing it. Third, all exposures were re-estimated under estimators with orthogonal robustness assumptions (weighted median, contamination mixture, MR-RAPS, CAUSE, MRlap), with concordant results. We therefore interpret these estimates as effects of the behaviour cluster the instruments index, not of a molecularly isolated exposure.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`For coffee, the literature is mixed. A previous, smaller MR study reported no causal coffee–AF association [8]; observational, acute-exposure randomized and recent genetic evidence points to no harm [4,9,30,31], although healthy-user bias may colour inverse estimates [10]. Our nominal IVW association does not overturn that consensus: it was heterogeneous, non-robust to sensitivity estimators, attenuated once BMI was modelled jointly and source-dependent. Colocalization reinforced this reading: no AF counterpart at the CYP1A1–CYP1A2 and AHR loci (PP.H4 ≤ 0.008), a shared causal variant with AF at FTO, and null caffeine-metabolism-locus instruments while the remainder carried the signal. Consumption-based instruments index intake behaviour rather than circulating caffeine [32], and plasma-caffeine MR links caffeine itself to lower adiposity [33]; the adiposity pathway captured here therefore reflects beverage-drinking patterning, not caffeine pharmacology. On balance, the genetic evidence establishes no causal coffee–AF effect in either direction, although heterogeneity precludes a definitive null. This aligns with the 2026 American Heart Association statement, which left caffeine-metabolism effect modification unresolved [10]; our locus-level analyses speak to that gap.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Tea is less studied: one Chinese cohort linked low-dose green tea to lower incident AF [34]; our null tea estimates extend that literature causally and concur with a previous null MR of tea intake against cardiovascular outcomes [41].`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Mechanisms: the role of adiposity`),
  mix([
    T`The BMI positive control (OR 1.47 per SD) reaffirms adiposity as a central causal determinant of AF and offers a parsimonious explanation for the observational literature: habitual SSB intake promotes weight gain, and the resulting adiposity, rather than the beverage per se, increases AF risk via atrial remodelling, epicardial fat and hypertension. Mediation MR quantified this pathway, FTO colocalization corroborated it, and the positive genetic correlations of SSB and ASB intake with AF (rg = 0.24–0.31, q < 0.05) alongside null MR estimates exemplify shared architecture without a causal beverage effect. Preventive emphasis therefore shifts towards weight management and dietary pattern [1].`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Implications`),
  mix([
    T`Most fundamentally, the component-proxy design estimates the marginal, lifelong effects of individual ingredients rather than the composite beverage: a null for sugar, caffeine and tea does not entail a null for milk tea, which additionally entails possible sugar–caffeine interaction, non-instrumented constituents and an acute per-serving character outside the lifelong estimand. The acute window and dose–response shape are questions for randomized ambulatory-monitoring designs [30].`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`Current genetic evidence does not support sweetened beverage intake as a direct causal determinant of AF; it offers no direct evidence implicating milk-tea-type beverages, but cannot exclude effects of the magnitude reported observationally. At the trigger level, randomized acute coffee exposure found no excess atrial arrhythmias [30], although case reports link high-dose energy drinks to acute AF in young, low-risk individuals [35]; our null accords with US guideline advice against recommending caffeine avoidance to prevent AF [36]. This is not a verdict of safety: the cardiometabolic case for limiting added sugar and sweeteners rests on evidence far beyond AF [38], and for young East Asian consumers the AF-prevention message remains healthy weight, limited alcohol and cardiometabolic risk control [1]. Prospective direct studies of milk tea in East Asian cohorts remain a priority.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Strengths and limitations`),
  mix([
    T`Strengths include multiple complementary estimators, the largest public European-ancestry AF GWAS, pre-specified multiplicity control, multivariable modelling and colocalization, a positive lifestyle control and an East Asian contextual arm. Several limitations merit emphasis. First, no GWAS measures milk tea itself: the component-proxy strategy assumes instrumented sweetening behaviour approximates the exposure of interest, ordinal phenotypes scale estimates per intake category, and MR indexes lifelong average exposure, so acute triggering, post-ablation recurrence and any J-shaped dose–response lie outside the estimand [10]. Second, sub-phenotype instruments used a relaxed threshold (P < 5 × 10⁻⁶); although all F statistics exceeded 20 (Table S22), F captures neither winner's curse nor correlated pleiotropy, and two sub-phenotypes lack public full summary statistics; the concordant MR-RAPS (Table S23) and MRlap estimates (difference P > 0.05; Table S17) mitigate, but do not eliminate, this concern. Third, instruments derive from European-ancestry adults whereas target consumers are young East Asians; the BioBank Japan analysis is subject to cross-ancestry mismatch, and the early-onset analysis was underpowered [37]. Fourth, diagnostic codes under-detect paroxysmal AF, and the FinnGen I9_AF endpoint pools AF with atrial flutter (ICD-10 I48). Fifth, UK Biobank contributes to both the exposure GWAS and Nielsen 2018; with F > 20 overlap bias should be minimal, and the LDSC intercept for coffee × Nielsen 2018 (−0.011) was null whereas it detected known coffee–tea overlap (−0.318). Finally, multivariable estimates rest on conditionally modest instruments (conditional F 1.3–7.5; Table S24), mediation MR was a mechanistic probe under product-method assumptions, and the deferred NHANES triangulation would have been uninformative by design.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Conclusions`),
  mix([
    T`Genetically proxied consumption of sweetened beverages—including the sugar component of tea- and coffee-based drinks, the closest available proxy for milk-tea-type beverages—showed no evidence of a causal association with atrial fibrillation in European or East Asian populations: moderate-to-large effects are excluded, but small effects, and milk-tea exposure itself, remain untested. The modest observational association is more plausibly explained by residual confounding, including adiposity, than by a direct causal effect of beverage sugar. Prevention is better directed at established cardiometabolic risk factors; direct investigation of milk tea consumption in prospective East Asian cohorts is needed.`,
  ])
);

